rkllm-runtime (@VERSION@) unstable; urgency=medium

  [ 1.3.1-17 audit remediation, reliability and observability ]
  * OpenAI compatibility: finish_reason=length with exact usage from the
    runtime end frame; streaming /v1/completions (text_completion envelope,
    include_usage); /health stats counters restored; ignored/unsupported
    request fields are accepted by the schema and dispatched through caps
    instead of being rejected by pydantic (store/metadata/etc.).
  * Streaming: remove the heartbeat-throttling bug, use a two-stage cancel
    and backpressure truncation, and record TTFT/duration/token metrics on
    streaming paths as well as non-streaming ones.
  * Context budget: prompt+max_tokens are clamped to the model context; an
    overlong prompt returns 400 instead of letting rkllm_run return -1.
    model.max_tokens_limit makes the request cap deployment-configurable;
    clamped responses carry X-RKLLM-Max-Tokens-Clamped.
  * Worker heartbeat: the worker emits heartbeat frames during inference; the
    supervisor kills a worker whose C call is stuck (process alive but no
    heartbeat), releasing the NPU; the next request restarts it. New
    engine.heartbeat_interval_s / heartbeat_timeout_s keys (config 85 keys);
    frame protocol version 2.
  * Metrics: TTFT and queue-wait histograms plus max_tokens clamp and
    heartbeat-timeout counters; the last-TTFT gauge remains for compatibility.
  * Token estimates share one byte-based estimator between context budget and
    usage fallback; /v1/responses also exposes the clamp header.
  * release_check.py checks version/changelog/config/caps/deb consistency.
  * Robustness: client errors no longer restart the worker; prompt cache
    moves to memory with byte-LCP + similarity; tool-call content and batch
    truncation edge cases fixed.
  * Engineering: single-source version (DEFAULT_VERSION), dead-code cleanup,
    route/pipeline shared layer, and an expanded selftest covering the HTTP
    surface.
  * Responses: streaming backpressure truncation is reported as
    response.incomplete with incomplete_details.reason=max_output_tokens
    instead of a false completed; non-streaming Responses builds the
    pipeline once (no duplicate prompt construction).
  * MCP: 2026 result shaping (resultType, tools/list cache metadata) is
    centralized in protocol.py; the stdio transport no longer carries a
    version-specific branch.
  * MCP connector: extract job start and polling preserve structuredContent;
    failed jobs preserve isError, fixing MCP -32600.
  * [2026-10-27 audit fixes] streaming chat + tools no longer aborts with
    KeyError (nested tool_calls); queued disconnects no longer abort unrelated
    in-flight requests; backpressure truncation aborts the worker; worker
    aborted end frames surface as RequestAborted.
  * OpenAI contract: non-streaming reasoning_content, Retry-After HTTP header
    on 503, OpenAI-shaped 400 validation errors, Responses max_output_tokens
    as incomplete, Responses/NaN input validation, json_schema de-fencing.
  * Runtime/deb: legacy conffile keys (CONTEXT/MAX_NEW_TOKENS/QUIET/memory)
    take effect; rk3576 uses big cores; progress-based heartbeat can kill a
    stuck C call; prerm stops the service; verify_deb checks conffile keys
    one by one; crosscheck_deb works without arguments.
  * MCP gateway: synchronous cancel maps to cancelled instead of -32603;
    default estimates avoid permanent job mode; job timeout scales with the
    worst-case generation; input is budgeted against context_len; _inflight
    is cleared on success; empty reasoning answers return isError; invalid
    args return -32602.

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-16) unstable; urgency=medium

  [ New FastAPI stack (grey release, apt route) ]
  * Ship src/rkllm (config/caps/abi/worker/server/agent/mcp) under
    /usr/lib/rkllm/current and a /usr/bin/rkllm-serve entry point. The legacy
    /usr/bin/rkllm stays untouched; both service units can coexist, only one
    binds the port.
  * Third-party dependencies come from apt (python3-fastapi, python3-uvicorn,
    python3-pydantic on trixie) instead of vendored wheels - decided
    2026-10-21, pending on-board version verification before release.
  * /etc/rkllm/rkllm.toml joins the conffile set: 80-key TOML with startup
    validation, one-shot error listing and per-key source reporting
    (rkllm-serve config show/validate/docs).
  * rkllm-server-next.service: TimeoutStartSec=300 (27B cold load), graceful
    drain, explicit opt-in (not enabled by the package).

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-15) unstable; urgency=medium
  [ API server and CLI ]
  * Refuse chunked request bodies with 411 instead of reading them as empty.
    The API speaks HTTP/1.1 keep-alive but only understood Content-Length, so
    the undecoded chunk frames stayed on the socket and the next request on
    that connection parsed them as part of its own body.
  * Keep the function-tools buffers alive for the life of the engine. ctypes
    does not pin the memory behind the c_char_p arguments and the runtime may
    hold those pointers for later runs; they are now kept alongside the model
    path. An empty system prompt is replaced by a neutral placeholder, because
    the runtime treats an empty value as "reset the chat template" rather than
    "leave it alone". /health reports "function_tools", and the first request
    that enables tools logs that every later request on this handle is affected
    until the service restarts.
  * Restore the clocks when a run, chat session or serve instance exits. The
    upstream fix_freq_*.sh helpers only ever lock: cpuidle is disabled, the
    governors are pinned to userspace and the frequencies to their maximum,
    and nothing undoes it - a one-shot `rkllm run` left the board at full clocks
    with deep idle off until the next reboot. The sysfs knobs are now
    snapshotted before the helper runs and restored on exit. A helper that
    exits 0 without any governor actually switching to userspace is reported as
    suspect instead of "ok".
  * Harden the service unit: NoNewPrivileges, PrivateTmp, ProtectSystem=full,
    RestrictSUIDSGID and RestrictAddressFamilies. ProtectHome is deliberately
    left unset because models are commonly kept under /home or /root, and full
    rather than strict keeps /sys writable for the frequency helper.
  * postinst warns when /etc/default/rkllm-server carries an API key but is
    still world readable. The conffile ships 0644 and dpkg never changes the
    mode, so "typed the key, forgot to chmod" previously had no checkpoint.
  * Document that RKLLM_EXTRA_ARGS is expanded by /bin/sh, i.e. it is code and
    not a list of options. Only root can write that file, so it is not a
    privilege boundary, but the entry should not be able to surprise anyone.
  * Both manual pages use RKLLM_API_KEY in their first API example instead of
    --api-key, which the same page warns would be visible in ps.
  * The queue-full 503 now carries Retry-After: 5, matching the other
    back-pressure exit, the one that gives up waiting for the NPU. Two branches
    of the same status code that differed in headers forced every client to
    special-case one of them.
  * A non-streaming request aborts once its client has gone away. Such a
    request writes nothing while generating, so a disconnect used to stay
    invisible until the whole generation finished - the NPU and infer_lock
    remained busy producing an answer nobody would read. select(2) plus
    MSG_PEEK separates "idle" from "a pipelined request is waiting" without
    consuming those bytes, and is polled twice a second.
  * Self test: four new checks (chunked body refused, health reports
    function_tools, queue-full 503 carries Retry-After, non-streaming
    disconnect probe), and the NULL-probe stub learned about the _keep list.

  [ Test toolchain ]
  * Stress test: the prompt-budget guard now assumes 3 bytes per token, which
    is what the server enforces (the old x8 value was the revision-9 defect);
    the concurrency ladder crosses the accept-queue size so 503 shedding is
    actually reachable; endurance resets the KV cache between rounds so drift
    is not confounded with context growth; a chunked-body case was added.
  * Client simulator: bisects the exact rejection boundary for long pastes and
    polls /health.busy after a stream interrupt to measure how long the NPU
    keeps working on a request the user already abandoned.
  * Crash probe: an 8-way concurrency step was added next to the 4-way one.
  * Real-world run: HTTP 4xx is recorded as "rejected" and not retried, while
    connection loss is counted as "crashes"; each row keeps its recovery time,
    so the earlier "crashes=1" rows can no longer mean either condition.
  * Health check: the error counter is compared as a delta over the run instead
    of an absolute zero, and the template-pollution check resets the
    conversation first so history and template cannot be confused.
  * Orchestrator: exit codes 0 (clean) / 2 (finished with findings) / 1
    (a step failed), a per-step summary, and a watchdog so a hung child cannot
    wedge the run. The board client accepts https and reads RKLLM_API_KEY as
    well as RKLLM_KEY.
  * One HTTP layer for the whole toolchain (board_api.py) instead of seven
    hand-rolled clients that disagreed about percentiles and about whether an
    empty 200 from /health means alive. The scripts lost their numeric
    prefixes, so the modules can be imported rather than exec'd.
  * A new quality.py reports the shapes a machine can judge: finish_reason
    distribution, measured overrun, repetition/degeneration, mojibake,
    streaming-versus-blocking parity, and response attribution under
    concurrency (a response delivered to the wrong request). The report gained
    a section for them. Semantic quality still needs a human reader.

  [ Packaging ]
  * make_deb falls back to the repository layout when neither
    RKLLM_PACKAGING_DIR nor RKLLM_RELEASE_TARBALL is set; crosscheck_deb drops
    an import left over from the old package structure; .gitignore covers the
    build output directories.
  * The packaging tools no longer fail quietly. emit_tree refuses to write a
    regular file where a symlink was requested - the verification tree must
    describe the same package dpkg sees - and re-reads is_symlink(), because
    Windows can leave an unresolvable entry behind without raising. The ar
    header length is checked with a real raise instead of an assert that
    `python -O` strips. The foreign-architecture filter matches whole path
    segments rather than substrings, so a file that merely carries the marker
    in its name is no longer dropped from the package.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make the service configuration a dpkg conffile. /etc/default/rkllm-server
    was a plain file, so installing a new version silently replaced it and the
    operator's RKLLM_MODEL and RKLLM_API_KEY were gone - the one thing a
    configuration file must never do. dpkg now keeps local edits on upgrade.
  * The file is still deleted when the package is removed. dpkg keeps conffiles
    on a plain `remove` and deletes them only on `purge`, so postrm unlinks it
    itself, and the package's "removal leaves nothing behind" contract is
    unchanged.
  * Document every server parameter in that file, in English and in Chinese,
    each with an optional example that is commented out. `rkllm serve` now also
    takes every option from its own RKLLM_* environment variable, so a setting
    no longer has to be spelled out as a raw argument. A value that does not
    parse is reported and ignored instead of aborting the argument parser,
    which used to show up in the journal as nothing but "status=2".
  * The text of the file no longer depends on the package version, so an upgrade
    cannot rewrite it and cannot reset a `chmod 600` applied because an API key
    lives in it. A self test and a packaging check lock both properties down.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report token usage with a language-independent estimate. The counts came
    from len(text.split()), which finds no spaces in Chinese and therefore
    reported a 15-token question as "1" - wrong by roughly an order of magnitude
    for every Chinese request, in the field clients use to budget cost and
    context. The new estimate is byte-based and identical to the one the
    prompt-size guard already used.
  * Add a self test for it. The previous usage check only verified the numbers
    were non-zero and internally consistent, and wrong values satisfied that
    too; the new one fails on any space-free input that is under-counted.
  * Document the sampling options (--top-k, --top-p, --temperature,
    --repeat-penalty) and --json in --help; they previously appeared as bare
    flags with no explanation of what they do or which way to turn them. The
    same for --model-dir and --lib under list/info/selftest, which define their
    own copies of those options.
  * Add docstrings to the public entry points (cmd_chat, cmd_run, cmd_serve,
    cmd_list, cmd_info, build_parser, main), the three request paths
    (_run_inference, do_POST, do_GET) and RealEngine.__init__, whose lifetime
    rule (one handle owns the KV cache for the whole process) was only implied
    by the surrounding code.
  * No behaviour change beyond the token estimate.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Serve requests whose system prompt does not fit the context window, instead
    of refusing them. An agent-style chat client sends tens of kilobytes of its
    own preamble - one such client sent 57 KB alongside a two-character "hello",
    which no 4096-token window can hold, so the user could not even say hello.
    When the preamble is what overflows, it is now dropped and the request is
    answered; the journal records its size. Requires the user's own text to fit.
  * The inference log now separates system and user byte counts, so it is
    immediately clear which part a request was made of. A bare "prompt 57283
    bytes" did not say whether the user pasted a document or the client attached
    an enormous preamble.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the service being killed by a large prompt. The prompt guard allowed
    eight bytes per token, so a 32 KB English prompt (roughly 8000 tokens)
    passed a 4096-token window check and the runtime overran its KV cache and
    died with SIGSEGV. The guard now assumes three bytes per token, the safe
    side for both English (about 4) and Chinese (about 3). Found by stress
    testing: a 32668-byte prompt took the whole service down.
  * Prompt and generation now share the context window: a max_tokens that does
    not fit alongside the prompt is trimmed to what remains, so a long prompt
    gets a shorter answer instead of a crash.
  * Raise the listening socket's accept queue from socketserver's default of 5
    to 256. A burst of 60 concurrent requests produced 15 client-side connection
    errors and almost no 503s, because the kernel dropped connections before
    they reached the queue logic that is meant to shed load.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Record every rejected request in the journal with its reason, and make the
    "no usable text" reply name what actually arrived (per-message role, content
    type and length). A chat UI turns our 400 into "the model is unavailable",
    so a client probing a model with an empty message - content: null, which
    several UIs send from an empty input box - was indistinguishable from a
    genuinely broken model. The journal line now says which it is.
  * The rejection counter now covers all invalid requests, not only the empty
    content case, so /health reports a truthful number.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Answer requests that carry no user turn instead of rejecting them. Clients
    probe a newly configured model with a system-only (or assistant-only)
    request, and the old "no user text found in 'messages'" reply made a
    perfectly working model look broken in the client's UI (Dify and friends
    surface the 400 as "the model is unavailable"). The last message carrying
    any text is now used as the prompt.
  * A system-only request is no longer prepended to itself, so the text reaches
    the model exactly once.
  * Fix an AttributeError (which surfaced as HTTP 500) when a client sent the
    system prompt in the multimodal content shape - a list of parts - instead of
    a plain string.
  * Log the shapes of the messages whenever a request is rejected for lack of
    usable text, so the journal alone shows what the client sent.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the service failing with exit code 2 and nothing useful in the journal.
    systemd expands an unset variable into an EMPTY ARGUMENT, so the bare
    ${RKLLM_PLATFORM_ARG} / ${RKLLM_EXTRA_ARGS} in ExecStart passed two stray
    empty arguments to rkllm, which argparse rejected as unrecognised
    positionals. ExecStart is now wrapped in /bin/sh, where an unquoted empty
    variable disappears instead of becoming an empty argument.
  * rkllm itself now ignores empty arguments and writes the "no command given"
    message to stderr, so the reason reaches the journal.
  * The self test no longer aborts silently when a check triggers sys.exit();
    it reports the abort and still prints the summary.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The systemd unit no longer guards on ConditionPathExists=/dev/rknpu. Drivers
    from 0.9.x onwards register the NPU as a DRM render node and do NOT create
    that path, so the guard silently skipped the unit on perfectly working
    boards - "skipped, unmet condition check" - and the service never started.
    Whether an NPU is usable is decided by the runtime at startup, and
    StartLimitBurst keeps a board without one from restarting forever.
  * `rkllm info` and `rkllm selftest` now look for the driver through several
    signals instead of one path: /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version, a DRM render
    node whose driver link points at rknpu, the loaded kernel module, and the
    legacy /dev/rknpu device node.
  * Documentation, README.Debian and both manual pages corrected: they no
    longer claim the runtime needs /dev/rknpu.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop calling rkllm_set_chat_template to install a system prompt. That call
    disables the model's built-in chat template parsing - the runtime warns
    about this - after which the model receives bare text without any of its
    special tokens: it rambles, answers as if continuing a document, and often
    never emits EOS, which the user sees as a request that hangs. The setting
    persists for the whole handle, so a single system message poisoned every
    later request until the service was restarted.
  * System text (both --system and a request-level system message) is now
    prepended to the prompt, which leaves the built-in template intact.
  * New self test check `system message reaches the model` locks this down.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report prompt/completion token counts in the response usage block. They were
    always zero, so chat clients displayed "0 tokens" for every answer.
  * The counts remain whitespace estimates: no tokeniser is available in this
    script, which the manual page and the README say explicitly.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a segmentation fault that killed the API server: rkllm_set_chat_template
    was called with NULL for the prompt prefix and postfix, which the runtime
    dereferences unconditionally. Any client that sends a system message (which
    is every chat UI) crashed the process.
  * Refuse prompts far larger than the model's context window with HTTP 400
    instead of letting the runtime run off the end of its KV cache.
  * Log the request size before inference starts, so the last line before a
    crash identifies the offending request.
  * Accept the API paths with and without the /v1 prefix, and explain the
    expected base URL in 404 responses and in `rkllm serve --help`.
  * /health now reports the model name, queue length and uptime.
  * Require the API key through $RKLLM_API_KEY or --api-key, compared in
    constant time, and describe it in the manual page.
  * Add English and simplified Chinese manual pages (/usr/share/man/man1 and
    /usr/share/man/zh_CN/man1).
  * Add rkllm-server.service and /etc/default/rkllm-server; installed but never
    enabled or started automatically. postinst restarts a service that was
    already running, prerm stops it, postrm drops any enable symlink.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian packaging built from the upstream rknn-llm release tarball.
  * Files relocated from the upstream SDK tree to FHS/multiarch paths.
  * Example sources installed read-only; see /usr/share/doc/@PACKAGE@/README.Debian.
  * New: /usr/bin/rkllm, a front end for local chat, one-shot inference and an
    OpenAI-compatible API server. Standard library only: no compilation step on
    the board and no pip packages.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@
